; The three-point stencil — the first program integer arithmetic makes possible.
;
; g7's kernel is dst[i] = (src[i-1] + src[i] + src[i+1]) / 3, and until now it
; could not be written at ALL: every arithmetic primitive was f64, so the index
; bound by fold-range was a value nothing could compute with. `(aindex a (sub i 1))`
; was inexpressible. See docs/spec/inventory.md §1.2.
;
; This is the reduction half of it, which needs the integer arithmetic and not
; the two things still missing — division, and storing into an array.
;
; Note what the window costs: three reads per iteration at three different
; offsets, which is exactly the shape a bounds-check refinement would have to
; reason about, and exactly the shape a compiler cannot prove safe on its own.

(use num/f64)
(use num/int)

(def window (fn (a j)
  (f64.add (f64.add (aindex a j)
                    (aindex a (int.add j 1)))
           (aindex a (int.add j 2)))))

(fn (a) (fold-range 0.0 (int.sub (alen a) 2)
          (fn (acc j) (f64.add acc (window a j)))))
